WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
You know, there is this deeply
primal, I mean almost automatic

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.720
ritual we all go through when
we walk into a new restaurant.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Oh, absolutely.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.920
You look up at the menu board, you see a
glowing, beautiful photograph of a burger

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.160
or like a steaming bowl of pasta, and
your mouth immediately starts to water.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.340
Right.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.160
It's just biology at that point.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.380
Exactly.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.040
It's a biological guarantee.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.560
You trust your eyes, your eyes
tell your stomach what's coming,

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.860
and the social contract of
dining is officially signed.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.600
It is arguably one of the oldest forms
of visual communication we have.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.640
You see food, you desire
food, you acquire it.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.640
But lately, I mean you look
a little closer at that menu

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:43.960
board and suddenly that primal
ritual just totally shatters.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.340
Yeah, things get weird.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:46.680
Really weird.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.200
The burger bun has the
texture of like a reptile.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.160
The pasta looks like perfectly
arranged yellow yarn.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.840
Oh, the yarn pasta is everywhere now.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.140
It is.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.580
And you realize with
this creeping sense of

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.140
horror that the food you're
looking at isn't food

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.940
at all it's what the internet has affectionately

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:07.600
dubbed AI food slop such a visceral term,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.440
but so accurate right and to figure out
why this visual catastrophe is suddenly

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.720
taking over the world Well, we've pulled
together a wild stack of sources today.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.020
It is quite the mix.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:21.380
Yeah We are looking at a
frustrated Stockholm food creator

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.060
on Instagram a highly engaged
and honestly hilarious

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.840
Swedish reddit thread alongside
global reporting from CNN,

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.200
The Guardian, and a piece
from The New York Times.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.240
Yeah, that New York Times piece was
actually an interactive feature.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.380
It was incredibly elusive during
our research because it was

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.080
locked behind a publisher
anti-bot Wall, ironically enough.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.340
Which is just too perfect.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:46.520
I know, right?

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.800
But it's titled alone, Why
AI Food Slop Looks So Gross.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.020
I mean, that sets the
perfect stage for what

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.800
we are unraveling today in the deep dive.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.080
It really does.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.800
Because our mission today is
to figure out why struggling

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.360
restaurant owners are willingly
adopting these alien images.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.360
Why our human brains react with such
intense, physical disgust to them.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.020
And what this bizarre trend means for the
future of our trust in the food we buy.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.220
It's a massive shift in how we
interact with the culinary world.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.480
Exactly.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.460
So whether you are a massive
foodie, tech enthusiast,

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.380
or just someone trying
to grab a quick lunch.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:22.840
Stick around.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.040
We are about to completely change how you

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.320
look at the menus in
your own neighborhood.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.060
Because we are looking at
a bizarre collision here.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.500
It's not just a story
about bad graphic design.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:34.620
No, not at all.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.560
It is a fundamental clash between
cutting edge, generative technology,

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.060
really desperate business economics,

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.820
and honestly our most ancient
evolutionary instincts.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.180
Let's start with the visual reality
of this phenomenon in the wild.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.700
Because we talking about slightly
over-edited photos here,

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.400
or someone going heavy
on an Instagram filter.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:55.920
Right, these aren't just touched up.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.720
No, we are talking about
absolute visual catastrophes.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:01.440
Take Adaeze Onejeme.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.840
She's a 20-year-old ad sales
worker highlighted in our sources.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.420
Yeah, the Hollywood Night Market story.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:07.640
Exactly.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.360
She was visiting a night
market in Hollywood,

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.080
which is a place you go specifically
for the vibrant food scene.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.720
And she noticed what she
called an AI flyer epidemic.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.120
An epidemic.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.100
Yeah, stall after stall, hawking
fries and smoothies that just

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:26.940
had this too perfect rubbery sort
of plastic aesthetic to them.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.760
The texture is almost
always the dead giveaway.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.180
It really is.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.840
Because generative AI
models notoriously struggle

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.240
with the organic unpredictable
chaos of real food.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.900
I mean think about it, a real potato
fry has a regular crispness, right?

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.220
Right, little burnt edges and stuff.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.100
Exactly salt granules
variations in color the

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.000
AI just smooths it all out into a uniform

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.700
rubbery cylinder But
honestly rubbery fries

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.920
are genuinely the least offensive example.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.360
We found for sure Let's
talk about Sam Biddle

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.840
He's a tech reporter who
took a photo at a New

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.580
York deli that went
completely viral and for all

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.760
the wrong reasons I
remember seeing this one.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:05.540
It's burned into my brain.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:10.000
It was a menu board advertising
a shrimp scampi dish But when

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.520
you looked at the shrimp, they
just, they weren't shrimp.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.340
No, not at all.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.860
There were these identical
perfectly circular

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.560
striped donut rings lying
on a bed of noodles.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.460
Biddle literally compared them to cosmic
monsters from an HP Lovecraft novel.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.800
It's so funny, but also so true.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.500
He called the whole image spiritually
unsettling and cosmically unnerving.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.860
I saw that photo and it is a profound
failure of the AI generation process.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.200
How does it even mess up that badly?

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.420
Well, the algorithm
understands that shrimp have

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.280
stripes and they curl
into a sea shape, right?

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:51.060
But it lacks the contextual understanding
of what a crustacean actually is.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:52.900
Right, it doesn't know
it's a living animal.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.300
Exactly.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.220
So it just connects the ends
and gives you a striped donut.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:01.260
And the scary thing is this isn't
an isolated one-off glitch.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.520
Consider Jill Sennett, a nurse in Denver,

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.300
she went to a Jamaican barbecue
pop-up at her hospital.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.460
Oh this one genuinely
made my stomach turn.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:11.560
It's really rough The
menu image for the meat

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.940
combo didn't look like
slow-cooked pork or beef.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:17.280
No, it looked awful It
looked like dark glossy

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.660
stitched leather belts and The texture was

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:22.220
rendered in such a way
that the meat appeared

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:23.980
to be crawling with tiny black beetles.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.980
Oh It was incredibly unappetizing It's
like being in a nightmare, isn't it?

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:34.280
The kind of dream where everything seems
perfectly normal until you look closer and

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.200
you realize like the hot dog has six ends
and the mustard is floating in midair.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:39.800
It really is dream logic.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.360
And speaking of hot dogs,
we have to spend some

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.040
time on the Reddit
thread from our sources.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.540
Over on the r/sweden

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.300
subreddit, a user posted a
photo from a street food counter

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.280
at a massive ICA Maxi supermarket
in a town called Skellefteå.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:59.940
This specific example is,
it's just a master class

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.860
in AI hallucination and
overwhelming visual clutter.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:04.140
It's a mess.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.880
It's not just the food
that the AI got wrong,

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:09.220
the entire context of the
image is deeply confused.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:12.840
I am still struggling to understand
how this got printed in a real store.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.320
It's this animated fantasy-themed
digital menu board.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.920
The background is a busy landscape of
like waterfalls and glowing lanterns.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:22.940
For a hot dog stand?

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.920
Yeah, but the AI
hallucinated these blatant

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:28.560
knockoff Pokémon characters
into the food scene.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:29.840
Just completely out of nowhere.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.380
Yeah, there's a yellow creature that looks

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.440
like a mutated hybrid of Pikachu and Pichu

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.700
There is a fiery monkey
resembling Infernape

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.760
and a blue penguin looking like Piplup

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.500
And on top of that the
text has these bizarre

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.660
spelling errors, right instead of Chicago

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:48.460
broad for the hot dog bun It says Chicago

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:51.940
board the reddit
commenters were absolutely

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:56.200
losing their minds over it I can imagine as
though we're pointing out the terrifying

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.340
copyright nightmare of
inadvertently stealing

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.360
the Nintendo's intellectual property While

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:04.420
others were just complaining that looking
at the menu felt like staring at a chaotic

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:08.020
illegal online casino
game The sheer scale of

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:10.120
the disconnect is what's
so fascinating here.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:11.220
What do you mean?

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.220
Well, you have a business owner
whether it's a small hospital

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.160
pop-up in Denver or a massive
supermarket chain in Sweden And they

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.380
are looking at these cosmic horrors
They are looking at leather

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:25.880
belts and mutant casino Pokemon,
and they are signing off on it.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:28.780
Yeah hitting print They are
looking at this and thinking,

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:31.060
yes, this will make people hungry.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:33.080
This is an asset to my business.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.760
I just cannot wrap my
head around that logic.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:39.280
I mean, if an AI menu makes a
customer physically recoil,

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.520
how on earth is this considered
a smart business move?

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:46.720
Why aren't owners just printing out a
piece of paper with Times New Roman Text?

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:50.380
To understand that you have to
look at the brutal realities

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:52.880
of the restaurant industry
right now, it's tough.

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:54.380
How tough are we talking?

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:57.280
According to a 2026
report from the National

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.580
Restaurant Association, 42% of U.S.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:03.220
operators failed to turn a profit in 2025.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:05.160
42%.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:05.680
Wow.

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.020
Almost half.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:06.640
That is insane.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:08.720
The margins are razor thin.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.780
Food costs are high, labor costs are
high, and rent is just astronomical.

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:15.780
Owners are desperate to save
money wherever possible.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:17.380
Right.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.340
So marketing is the first
thing to get caught.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:19.760
Exactly.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.200
Exactly.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:23.180
Hiring a professional food
photographer can easily cost hundreds,

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.660
if not thousands of dollars
for a single menu refresh.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:28.920
That's why the NRA found
that 26% of restaurant

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:31.760
owners are now using AI,
primarily for marketing.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.020
Okay, so it's a desperation play.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:35.380
Yes.

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:37.400
So for an exhausted owner
who is just trying to keep

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.680
the lights on and get a
sign printed by Tuesday,

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:45.000
typing a prompt into ChatGPT isn't some
malicious attempt to deceive the public.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:46.260
It's a survival tactic.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:47.460
That makes sense.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.100
It's free.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:49.200
It's fast.

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:52.620
And to an untrained eye, when you're
stressed, it might look good enough.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:55.040
And some of them are just
doing it reluctantly, right?

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.740
Like, some owners actively
love the aesthetic.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:58.920
Oh, absolutely.

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:03.960
The sources highlight Nila Norero Salvatore,
who owns Bella Cafe in San Francisco.

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:07.140
She used ChatGPT to
design these incredibly

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.720
elaborate spaghetti
Western-themed breakfast menus.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.020
Which is a fun concept.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:13.380
It is.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:16.980
We're talking dishes named The Magnificent
Seven and A Fistful of Dollars.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.220
But they're all accompanied
by these hyper glossy,

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:24.880
warm-toned AI generated photos of eggs

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:28.000
and bacon that look like oil paintings.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:29.320
Very stylized.

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:32.220
Yeah, and she told reporters that
experimenting with AI is fun and

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:35.080
she genuinely thinks it's a brilliant
way to stand out and advertise.

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:39.280
The problem with that strategy though
is the massive consumer backlash.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.540
Diners aren't finding it fun,
they are finding it insulting.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:43.920
They really hate it.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.820
and it is resulting in
real-world financial

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:47.740
damage for the businesses
that lean into it.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.460
Take another San Francisco cafe mentioned
in The Guardian, Grind & Unwind.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:53.860
Oh, the vandalism story.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:57.060
Yes, they put up a sign with
AI-generated menu options.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:00.460
Locals immediately identified
the rubbery textures and the

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:04.200
surreal lighting as AI, and
the storefront was vandalized.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:05.860
Just because of the menu.

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:08.520
Yeah, someone took a spray
can and painted the word

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:11.680
seriously in massive letters
right over the AI signs.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.140
The owner had to spend an estimated $700
just to clean and paint over the graffiti.

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:18.860
Which completely wipes
out any money they saved

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:20.860
by not hiring a photographer
in the first place.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:22.600
I mean, the irony is just painful.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:24.360
It really is a backfire.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.380
Especially when you see the exact opposite

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:29.700
approach going totally
viral for being authentic.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:31.660
Right, the Wyoming story.

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:34.200
Yeah, a place called
Chugwater Soda Fountain

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.140
in Wyoming posted a picture on Instagram.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:37.960
It wasn't a polished photo at all.

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:40.220
It's just a piece of cardboard
where they hand wrote a

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.900
pledge to never use AI in their
marketing just a sharpie on

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:48.600
cardboard Exactly and that
simple Scrawled cardboard sign

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.900
got two hundred thousand
likes people are starving for

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:55.580
authenticity That contrast perfectly
illustrates the disconnect

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:58.400
here Restaurant owners have
been told for decades by

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.100
marketing gurus that people
eat with their eyes first So

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:04.740
they feel immense pressure to
have a highly visual menu.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:08.860
Yeah, they feel like they need photos
to survive But they are fundamentally

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:13.220
misunderstanding the psychology of
how humans process images of food.

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:14.700
Actually, let me stop you there.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:19.020
Because the psychology of this is
where the deep dive gets really wild.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:20.400
It gets deep.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:24.320
Because the reaction from customers isn't
just, oh, that's tacky graphic design.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:25.620
I'm going to take my business elsewhere.

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:27.420
The reaction is visceral.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:28.920
Very much so.

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:32.340
In that Swedish Reddit thread,
multiple people literally

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:34.660
said looking at the menu
made them want to throw up.

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:38.500
Why is a picture of a
slightly plastic looking

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.320
piece of quiche triggering
a biological gag reflex?

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:44.800
Well, it comes down to a
very specific psychological

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.880
phenomenon intersecting with
our evolutionary biology.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:51.040
The first piece of the puzzle
is the Uncanny Valley.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.220
Okay, the Uncanny Valley.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.480
Yeah, this was a concept
introduced way back in

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:58.820
1970 by a Japanese roboticist
named Masahiro Mori.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:01.540
I've heard of this, but it's almost always

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:04.580
applied to robots or bad
CGI in movies, isn't it?

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:08.300
Like when a human face in a video
game looks almost photorealistic,

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:13.140
but the eyes are completely dead, and
it just gives you the creeps Exactly.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:17.900
Mori observed that as a robot becomes
more human-like, our empathy and

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:23.040
affinity toward it increases up to a
specific point Okay, but right before it

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:27.540
reaches perfect realism, any slight
imperfections make it look like a moving

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:32.280
corpse or a zombie That triggers a
sudden violent plunge in our affinity.

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:34.720
That plunge is the Uncanny Valley.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:36.840
That makes sense for faces.

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.000
But what researchers are
now realizing is that the

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.780
Uncanny Valley absolutely
applies to our food, too.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:47.340
Alexander Diel, a psychologist at the
University of Duisburg-Essen in Germany,

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:50.220
he conducted a peer-reviewed
